 ## Overview
-Image Enhancement and Restoration Methods address the degradation of input imagery—such as blur, transient occlusions, or low resolution—by integrating restoration mechanisms directly into the NeRF optimization loop. Unlike standard NeRF which assumes sharp, clean inputs, these methods either model the degradation process (e.g., blur kernels) or separate static scene geometry from transient artifacts (e.g., pedestrians) to render clean novel views. They differ from sibling methods like [DeblurNeRF](11964-deblurnerf.md) and [IE-NeRF](13737-ie-nerf.md) by focusing on specific degradation types: optical blur, transient occlusions, or resolution limits, often using 2D priors (inpainting, super-resolution) to guide 3D consistency.
+Image Enhancement and Restoration Methods modify the NeRF training pipeline to handle input image defects such as blur, transient occlusions, or missing data, rather than modifying the scene representation itself [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that alter the MLP architecture or density representation (e.g., [DeblurNeRF](11964-deblurnerf.md), [Sharp-NeRF](14270-sharp-nerf.md), [IE-NeRF](13737-ie-nerf.md)), this family focuses on the supervision signal and loss computation, often integrating external 2D priors or explicit blur models to guide the optimization of a standard or slightly modified radiance field.
 
 ## Shared principles
-The standard NeRF architecture [S6] consists of two Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs): a coarse network and a fine network. The coarse network takes positional encoded position $\gamma(\mathbf{x})$ and outputs a latent feature vector $\mathbf{h}$ and volume density $\sigma$. The fine network takes $\mathbf{h}$, positional encoded position $\gamma(\mathbf{x})$, and positional encoded view direction $\gamma(\mathbf{d})$ to output color $\mathbf{c}$.
+The standard NeRF architecture [Parent](3065-nerf-hyperparameters.md) serves as the baseline for most modifications. It consists of two Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs): a coarse network and a fine network. The coarse network takes positional encoded position $\gamma(\mathbf{x})$ and outputs a latent feature vector $\mathbf{h}$ and volume density $\sigma$. The fine network takes $\mathbf{h}$, positional encoded position $\gamma(\mathbf{x})$, and positional encoded view direction $\gamma(\mathbf{d})$ to output color $\mathbf{c}$ [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
 
